# Staging: the production stack, on the production hostnames, without ACME.
#
# `tls internal` issues from Caddy's own CA instead of Let's Encrypt. That is
# the single difference from the production Caddyfile — the routing is imported
# from the same sites.caddy both use — so a pass here exercises everything a
# certificate cannot change:
#
#   * the API URL baked into the web bundle at build time, which is a build arg
#     and therefore untestable by editing environment variables
#   * CORS between two different hostnames, which localhost:5454 never exercised
#   * the Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy header that lets report photographs be
#     embedded across those two origins
#   * the WebSocket upgrade for the technician event stream
#
# The hostnames have to resolve to this machine. Add to the hosts file
# (C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts, or /etc/hosts):
#
#   127.0.0.1 inspection.texasrenters.com inspection-api.texasrenters.com
#
# Browsers will warn about the certificate, because Caddy's CA is not in the
# trust store. That is expected and is not the thing under test — click through,
# or install the root from the caddy-data volume at
# /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt.

import sites.caddy

{$WEB_HOST} {
	tls internal
	import console
}

{$API_HOST} {
	tls internal
	import api
}
